# Bashbot Implementation

Bash bot allows piping together agents in bash. It does this using stdout and stdin.

## Anatomy of a bash bot

```bash
agent [name] < stdin > stdout
```

- The `stdin` can be of other agents, test runner or files. It is just stdin.
- The agent_fn can be named `code` or `review` or any role you think it needs. I like to use verbs.
- An agent writes to stdout for other agents to use or for you to read.
- The agent can have a name so it re-uses its session.
- Some say it is good to output structured data. I decided on a very minimal xml.

We will prefix functions with a `_underscore` to denote it is an internal. We'll try and keep that to a minimum.

## Exit Codes

We use the exit code 22 to signal that the failure was because of an agent saying that it failed but within its domain.
We want literally any other exit code to result in the program exiting and just stop.

```bash
feedback() {
  code | review || goto feedback
}
```

# Goto

The `divert` function is a little smart. It will only divert if exit code was 22 and otherwise it will not call `feedback`.
Furthermore it's important that when a review fails 22 that the coder gets the result of the review. In this case it's pretty clear because it
will receive that from the output of the review step. But what if the code fails. We need the coder to get its own feedback in that case.
We can't have the stdin skip the review.


```bash woven divert
export BASHBOT_MAX_RETRIES=${BASHBOT_MAX_RETRIES:-10}

divert() {
  local exit_code="$?"

  if [ "$exit_code" -ne 22 ]; then
    return "$exit_code"
  fi

  local BASHBOT_RETRY_COUNT="${BASHBOT_RETRY_COUNT:-0}"

  if [ "$BASHBOT_RETRY_COUNT" -gt "$BASHBOT_MAX_RETRIES" ]; then
    echo "bashbot: max retries ($BASHBOT_MAX_RETRIES) exceeded for call args ($*), aborting loop" >&2

    # Exit with a 22 but do no continue the loop. Outside loops can delegate more loops if desired.
    return 22
  fi

  recollect | BASHBOT_RETRY_COUNT="$((BASHBOT_RETRY_COUNT + 1))" "$@"
}


# aliases
goto() { divert "$@"; }
redirect() { divert "$@"; }
```

## Collections

You must have wondered what the `collect` does in the code you saw previously.

At any moment memories can be collected to be recollected or forgotten at a later point to likely share with other agents or
for reviewing or debugging the pipelines.

```bash
# Usage

code-review() {
  code | collect "iter-${1:-0}" | review || goto code-review "$(("${1:-1}" + 1))"
}

code-review | collect final-result

recollect final-result
recollect iter-0 # you can review the iterations
recollect iter-1
```

By default a new collection is created every time the script is run. If you do not want that you can reference a specific collection
with a name

```bash
collection; # Creates a new collection

collection plan_phase # Will allow one to recollect items from that collection.

recollect # Will recollect the last item pushed here. A recollect without an arg has special meaning because agents push here by default.
```

```bash woven collection
# shellcheck disable=SC2016
export BASHBOT_COLLECTION_FILEPATH_TEMPLATE='$HOME/.config/bashbot/collection/${BASHBOT_COLLECTION_NAME:-$RANDOM}'

# shellcheck disable=SC2120
collection() {
  local BASHBOT_COLLECTION_NAME="$1"
  eval "export BASHBOT_COLLECTION_FILEPATH=$BASHBOT_COLLECTION_FILEPATH_TEMPLATE"
}

# shellcheck disable=SC2120
collect() {
  local name="${1:-}"
  tee "$BASHBOT_COLLECTION_FILEPATH$name" | sponge
}

forget() {
  local name="${1:-}"
  true > "$BASHBOT_COLLECTION_FILEPATH$name"
}

# shellcheck disable=SC2120
recollect() {
  local name="${1:-}"
  cat "$BASHBOT_COLLECTION_FILEPATH$name"
}

# recollect alias
recall() { recollect "$@"; }
```


## Sessions

Agents tend to have a session or conversation history. This is managed by the code agent.

We want to be able to say when an agent should re-use its session or create a new one.
Just like with the memories.

```bash
# Example to create a new one off session within the subshell.
# The reviewer outside the subshell will not use that session. It will keep using the one it has.
(session; code-review-loop) | review

# Create a named session. Subsequent runs will re-use that session for the code-review-loop.
(session develop; code-review-loop) | review
```

```bash woven session
# shellcheck disable=SC2016
export BASHBOT_SESSION_FILEPATH_TEMPLATE='$HOME/.config/bashbot/session/${BASHBOT_SESSION_NAME:-$RANDOM}'

# shellcheck disable=SC2120
session() {
  # Either a named session or create a new one (randomly).
  local BASHBOT_SESSION_NAME="${1:-$RANDOM}"

  eval "export BASHBOT_SESSION_FILEPATH=$BASHBOT_SESSION_FILEPATH_TEMPLATE"
}
```

## Define a default agent

We will store this file in this repository. We will make it reflect what it will look like on the system.

```bash cat - > .config/bashbot/agent
#!/usr/bin/env bash

set -euo pipefail

filepath="$1"; shift # location of the role for bot

id=""
if [ -n "${2:-}" ]; then
  id="$2"
  shift 2
fi

filename="${filepath##*/}"

# shellcheck disable=1090
source "$filepath"

session="$BASHBOT_SESSION_FILEPATH$filename$id"

# Research if escaping text will confuse (smaller) models.
xml_wrap() {
  echo "<$1>"
  cat - # | jq -Rr '@html' # You can xml escape the contents so botty is less likely to get confused. I hope...
  echo "</$1>"
}

# Exits non zero if substring not found. Will still output all the stdin wrapped and then exit non zero.
contains() {
  local pattern="$1"
  local result=22

  while IFS= read -r line; do
    if [[ "$line" == *"$pattern"* ]]; then
      result=0
    fi
    echo "$line"
  done

  return $result
}

# Write this more concise
if [ -f "$session" ]; then
  # shellcheck disable=SC2154
  pi --system-prompt "$system" --print --resume --session "$session" "$@" | xml_wrap "role-$filename-$id" | contains "$success_flag"
else
  # shellcheck disable=SC2154
  pi --system-prompt "$system" --print --session "$session" "$@" | xml_wrap "role-$filename-$id" | contains "$success_flag"
fi
```

## Reload

Used to update the functions with changes in the roles directory.

```bash woven reload
export BASHBOT_ROLES_PATH="${BASHBOT_ROLES_PATH:-$HOME/.config/bashbot/roles}"

# Function Templating
# --------------

reload() {

  # shellcheck disable=SC1090
  source <(
    for filepath in "$BASHBOT_ROLES_PATH"/*; do
      log <<< "Loaded $filepath"
      # Get the role variables.
      local filename="${filepath##*/}"

      # Start the function body of the bot and a subshell
      # The subshell is to prevent the variables in the bot definition
      # file to leak out.
      # shellcheck disable=2016
      printf '%s() { "$BASHBOT_AGENT_FILEPATH" "%s" "$@" | tap-log; }; export -f "%s";\n' \
        "$filename" \
        "$filepath" \
        "$filename"
    done
  )
}
```

## When executed

```bash woven when_executed

# The bashbot executable was executed and not sourced
if [[ "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}" == "${0}" ]] && [[ ${#BASH_SOURCE[@]} -lt 2 ]]; then

    # Was called with a file so run it as a script
    test -f "$1" && {
      source bashbot

      # shellcheck disable=1090
      source "$1"
      exit "$?"
    }

    # Start an interactive thing
    bash --rcfile <(

      # Pipefail
      # --------
      echo 'set -o pipefail'

      # shellcheck disable=SC2016
      echo 'PS1="${BASHBOT_PS1:-"🤖 $PS1"}"'

      # Tell it to source itself
      echo 'source bashbot'
    )

    # The bash shell enhanced with bashbot has closed and we can now exit.
    # We exit because we don't don't need it to register the functions down there.
    exit
fi

# < when_sourced
```

## When sourced

```bash woven when_sourced
# < reload

reload
```

## Log util

```bash woven log_util
log() {
  while read -r line; do
    echo "[bashbot] $line" >&2
  done
}

tap-log() {
  while read -r line; do
    echo "[bashbot] $line" >&2
    echo "$line"
  done
}

export -f tap-log log
```

## Conclusion

```bash WOVEN_PREFIX='# < ' woven > ./bashbot
#!/usr/bin/env bash

# shellcheck disable=SC2034
test -z "$BASHBOT_DEBUG" || {
  set -x
}

# Log util
# --------

# < log_util

# When executed
# -------------

# < when_executed

# Collections
# -----------

# < collection

# Divert / Goto / Redirect
# ------------------------

# < divert

# Sessions
# --------

# < session

# Setup
# -----

export BASHBOT_AGENT_FILEPATH="${BASHBOT_AGENT_FILEPATH:-"$HOME/.config/bashbot/agent"}"

session
collection
```
